# SYNOPSIS

```swift
struct KMemStats: Equatable, Sendable {
  var total: UInt64
  var free: UInt64
  var wired: UInt64
  var heap: UInt64
  var heapFree: UInt64
  var vmo: UInt64
  var mmu: UInt64
  var ipc: UInt64
  var other: UInt64
  init(total: UInt64, free: UInt64, wired: UInt64, heap: UInt64,
       heapFree: UInt64, vmo: UInt64, mmu: UInt64, ipc: UInt64,
       other: UInt64)
  var used: UInt64 { get }
  static func read(_ resource: borrowing Handle) throws(Status) -> KMemStats
}

struct CPUStats: Equatable, Sendable {
  var cpu: UInt32
  var online: Bool
  var idleTime: Int64
  var contextSwitches: UInt64
  var interrupts: UInt64
  var timerInterrupts: UInt64
  var timers: UInt64
  var syscalls: UInt64
  var rescheduleIPIs: UInt64
  var genericIPIs: UInt64
  static var systemBase: UInt64 { get }
  static func read(_ resource: borrowing Handle, max: Int = 64)
    throws(Status) -> [CPUStats]
  var wakeups: UInt64 { get }
}

enum Power {
  static func reboot(_ resource: borrowing Handle) throws(Status)
  static func poweroff(_ resource: borrowing Handle,
                       sleepTypes: (UInt8, UInt8)?) throws(Status)
  static func warmboot(_ resource: borrowing Handle, kernel: borrowing Handle,
                       bootfs: borrowing Handle,
                       cmdline: borrowing Handle) throws(Status)
  static func warmboot(_ resource: borrowing Handle, kernel: borrowing Handle,
                       bootfs: borrowing Handle) throws(Status)
  static var systemBase: UInt64 { get }
}

enum Debuglog {
  @discardableResult
  static func waitForConsole(deadline: Int64) -> Bool
}

enum CPRNG {
  static func draw(count: Int) throws(Status) -> [UInt8]
}
```

# DESCRIPTION

## Memory

`KMemStats.read` returns a snapshot of the kernel's memory, in bytes, from `object_get_info(2)` on the resource passed.
That is the system resource that grants information (see `CPUStats.systemBase`).
`total` and `free` are the RAM the kernel manages and what is free of it; `wired`, `vmo`, `mmu`, `ipc` and `other` are bytes in use by kind, and `heap` and `heapFree` are the kernel heap's total and free bytes.
`used` is `total - free`, or 0 if `free` is larger.

## CPUs

`CPUStats.read` returns one record for each CPU, up to `max`, from the same resource.
`systemBase` is the base of the system resource that grants reading them.
A record holds the CPU's number, whether it is online, the nanoseconds it has been idle, and counts since boot: context switches, device and other interrupts, timer interrupts, timer callbacks run, system calls, and reschedule and generic inter-processor interrupts.
`wakeups` is the sum of the four interrupt counts, which on an idle machine are the times the CPU woke.

## Power

`Power.reboot` restarts the machine, given the power system resource.
`Power.poweroff` turns it off: on amd64 with ACPI's `\_S5` sleep types, `sleepTypes`, which an AML interpreter evaluates (devmgr(8)), failing with `notSupported` without them; on arm64 and rv64 through the firmware, needing none.
`Power.warmboot` hands the machine to another kernel without the firmware: the kernel ELF in `kernel` and the bootfs in `bootfs`, each as long as its content size (`VMO.setContentSize`, sys-vmo(2)), with the command line in `cmdline`, or this boot's without one.
The kernel checks and copies both, loads the new kernel at a new random base, builds the boot page tables and handoff the loader would, and only then stops: the other CPUs, the IOMMUs, interrupts. The new kernel then boots as from the loader.
A bad ELF, or too little memory for the copies, is refused with nothing stopped; a device still doing DMA must be stopped first (devmgr(8) clears bus mastering).
It runs only on amd64; elsewhere it is `notSupported`.
None of these returns if it succeeds.
All are `system_powerctl(2)`.
`Power.systemBase` is the system resource's base for the power resource, to make one from the system resource (sys-resource(2)).

## Debuglog

The debuglog is the kernel's log, a ring that every process's standard output writes to.
`waitForConsole` waits until the console has printed every record written up to the time of the call, or until the monotonic `deadline` (see sys-clock(2)).
It returns true once caught up, and false if the deadline came first, or if there is no console to ask.
It polls every 10 ms.

## Random bytes

`CPRNG.draw` returns `count` bytes from the kernel's cryptographic random number generator, for keys, nonces and the like.
It draws in pieces of at most 256 bytes, the limit of `cprng_draw(2)`, and throws the first failure.

